1. We still need to know whether one symmetric cone can be linearly
   isomorphic to another symmetric cone, but not Jordan-isomorphic to
   it.

   Lyapunov rank is defined for any convex cone, and we should be able
   to ask these same sorts of questions about proper or convex
   cones. But, the definition of similacrum INCLUDES the definition of
   "isomorphism." It's fine to restrict our attention to symmetric
   cones to make our job easier, but when we say "all isomorphism is
   Jordan isomorphism," we are not only restricting the cones under
   consideration, but also fundamentally changing the definition of a
   similacrum, even as it would apply to non-symmetric cones.

   If we can solve this problem, I'll need to go back and rewrite the
   paragraph about how all cone isomorphisms are Jordan
   isomorphisms. Or if we can't solve it, maybe it makes sense to
   change the definition of a similacrum to apply only to a symmetric
   cone.
